namespace eval Examples {
    variable _ruff_ns_opts {
        -heading {Examples}
    }
    variable _ruff_preamble {
        Examples in this section describes examples from folder `examples` in root directory of NgspiceTclBridge.
        List of availible tutorials:
        - [Parametric simulation] - "examples/parametric_simulation.tcl" file

        ## Parametric simulation
        In this example we alter diode model parameter `n` (emission coefficient) and observe how diode IV curve is
        changed from original.

        First step is to provide path to Ngspice library. In Linux case it is usually saved under `/usr/local/lib`, for
        Windows it depends on the installation prefix. Here we use the environmental variable `NGSPICE_DLL` to get the
        location:
        @include-code examples/parametric_simulation.tcl -region find-shared-library

        The circuit we simulate is defined by string:
        @include-code examples/parametric_simulation.tcl -region netlist
        
        #ruffopt excludedformats {nroff}
        ![diode_iv_cir](assets/img/diode_iv_cir.png)
        #ruffopt excludedformats {}

        Be carefull: for correct usage you have to provide `.end` statement at the the end of the netlist.

        Load ngspice library and initialize command for library control by [::ngspicetclbridge::new] command:
        @include-code examples/parametric_simulation.tcl -region create-handle

        Load circuit into simulation with [::ngspicetclbridge::SIM circuit] command:
        @include-code examples/parametric_simulation.tcl -region load-circuit

        By default subcommand `circuit` accepts list of lines where each line contains one line of netlist, but switch
        `-string` allows to pass circuit as a string with lines being delimited by newline symbol.

        Add list of `n` parameter values we want to simulate:
        @include-code examples/parametric_simulation.tcl -region set-n-values

        Then we run loop for each value of `n`, send `altermod` Ngspice command by [::ngspicetclbridge::SIM command],
        run simulation by [::ngspicetclbridge::run] command (procedure waits for the end of simulation), then
        read vectors with [::ngspicetclbridge::SIM asyncvector] command and prepare data for plotting:
        @include-code examples/parametric_simulation.tcl -region simulation-loop

        Subcommand `command` send the string to Ngspice as is. In this case we send `altermod` command (see Ngspice
        manual 13.5.4) that allows to alter parameter of particular model without resetting the circuit or
        circuit reparsing.

        The we use `ticklecharts` package to plot resulted curves:
        @include-code examples/parametric_simulation.tcl -region plot-ticklecharts

        #ruffopt excludedformats {nroff}
        !ticklechart_parametric_simulation!
        #ruffopt excludedformats {}
    }
}
